              factors for human coronary heart diseases. Here we report an
              increase of blood or heart eosinophil counts in humans and mice
              after myocardial infarction (MI), mostly in the infarct region.
              Genetic or inducible depletion of eosinophils exacerbates cardiac
              dysfunction, cell death, and fibrosis post-MI, with concurrent
              acute increase of heart and chronic increase of splenic
              neutrophils and monocytes. Mechanistic studies reveal roles of
              eosinophil IL4 and cationic protein mEar1 in blocking H 2 O 2 -
